  "text": "Solution 3 is the most likely correct one, though I could not verify it against the source paper.\n\n- **Solution 1 (p = 2):** The div-curl lemma only shows that a product of two weakly convergent L² sequences converges in the sense of distributions. It does not show that the nonlinear constraint det M = 0 survives weak limits, so the rigidity claim is unsupported. The Sobolev-type heuristic is not checked either.\n- **Solution 2 (p = 4/3):** The argument is a heuristic. It pairs W^{1,p} ⊂ L^{p*} with L^p and sets the exponent condition to the boundary case, but it never shows that the threshold is sharp for this inclusion. The claimed identification with the Hessian-determinant threshold is asserted, not derived.\n- **Solution 3 (p = 3/2):** It cites Breden, Lopes Filho and Nussenzveig Lopes on rigidity and flexibility for the steady Euler equations in the plane, and states the threshold 3/2 for the rigidity/flexibility dichotomy. I recall the pressureless steady-state result as having this threshold, but I cannot confirm the exact paper title or statement from memory, so my confidence is moderate.\n\nSince the problem asks for an exact value and the only candidate with a specific literature source gives 3/2, I select Solution 3.\n\nCHOICE: 3",
